{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 55,
      "why": "Pipelined gives 1 result/cycle with the shortest latency (N+2) and highest Fmax, the best chance to satisfy the 0.5 us burst bound; varying W and N trades area against the 2^-12 accuracy requirement."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 6, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 45,
      "why": "Registering every m stages cuts flip-flop count (area) and shortens cycle latency, but lengthens the combinational path; exploring m=2..6 finds the area/Fmax sweet spot for the min-area objective while still meeting the burst deadline."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The system constraint (32 results within 0.5 us of a 1 MHz tick) demands both high throughput and low latency, so only 1-result/cycle pipelined families are plausible. Pipelined offers the highest Fmax and lowest latency; pipelined_m trades Fmax for fewer registers and smaller area. We search W=14..20 and N=8..20 to cover the area-vs-accuracy Pareto front, with guard bits and rounding to reach max_abs_err <= 2^-12. The budget is split to map both families' fronts before the reserved full-range mapping round."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Feasible, not infeasible: 22 of 60 evaluations satisfy all three constraints and the Pareto front carries 6 of them, so no constraint is unreachable. Evidence: throughput_msps >= 32 is met with large margin (cheapest front design runs 128 MSPS vs the required 32; best seen 163); max_abs_err <= 2^-12 is met (front designs give 4.64e-05..1.9e-05, i.e. 14.4..15.7 accuracy bits, versus the required 12, so even the least accurate front point has ~2.4 bits of headroom); sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 is met (best seen 0.263 us, so even allowing for the L1 bound being optimistic the cheapest candidates have roughly a 2x margin before L2 re-selection could disqualify them). All six front designs are pipelined with data_width=20, n_iter 16..19, angle_guard 1, frac_guard 0..2, covering area_um2 49554..61871 against the HV reference of 100000 and accuracy 14.4..15.7 against the reference of 12 - a well-populated trade-off curve in both objectives. The selection rule (min area_um2) is served by the front's cheapest point, pipelined [W=20 N=16 aG=1 fG=0 round] at 49554 um^2, which itself passes every constraint with margin. This is the final round, and further evaluation cannot change the outcome, so stop."}
